---
title: Error Codes
description: Numeric error codes returned by NodeDB with descriptions and resolution hints.
---

# Error Codes

NodeDB uses `NodeDbError` — a struct with numeric `ErrorCode`, human-readable `message`, machine-matchable `ErrorDetails`, and optional chained `cause`.

## Error Categories

| Code Range | Category          | Examples                                  |
| ---------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| 1000–1099  | Write path        | Constraint violation, deadline            |
| 1100–1199  | Read path         | Collection not found, database not found  |
| 1200–1299  | Query             | Plan error, undefined function            |
| 1300–1399  | Engine            | Array engine error                        |
| 1400–1499  | Quota and overload | Quota exceeded, server overload          |
| 1500–1599  | Clone             | Clone depth exceeded                      |
| 1600–1699  | Move tenant       | Drain timeout, already at target          |
| 1700–1799  | Mirror            | Mirror read-only                          |
| 1800–1899  | Backup            | Tenant or key mismatch                    |
| 2000–2099  | Auth              | Authorization denied, tenant limits       |
| 3000–3099  | Sync              | Delta rejected, sync error                |
| 4000–4299  | Storage           | Segment corrupted, WAL error, codec error |
| 5000–5099  | Config            | Bad request, invalid config               |
| 6000–6099  | Cluster           | No leader, node unreachable               |
| 7000–7099  | Memory            | Memory exhausted                          |
| 8000–8099  | Encryption        | Key error, decrypt failure                |
| 9000–9099  | Internal          | Unexpected internal error                 |

## Common Errors

| Error                  | Code | Resolution                  |
| ---------------------- | ---- | --------------------------- |
| `COLLECTION_NOT_FOUND` | 1100 | Check collection name       |
| `TYPE_MISMATCH`        | 1020 | Check data types in query   |
| `CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION` | 1000 | Duplicate key on plain INSERT maps to SQLSTATE `23505` (`unique_violation`). Use `UPSERT` or `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING / DO UPDATE` for non-error semantics. |
| `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED`    | 1002 | Query took too long         |
| `INSUFFICIENT_BALANCE` | 1022 | Not enough for TRANSFER     |
| `OVERFLOW`             | 1021 | i64 overflow on INCR        |
| `AUTHORIZATION_DENIED` | 2000 | Check RBAC grants           |
| `SYNC_DELTA_REJECTED`  | 3001 | CRDT sync constraint failed |

## Database Boundary Errors

| Error | Code | Resolution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `DATABASE_NOT_FOUND` | 1110 | Verify database name; check `SHOW DATABASES` |
| `CANNOT_DROP_DEFAULT_DATABASE` | 1710 | The `default` database is reserved; drop user databases instead |

## Quota and Overload Errors (1400–1499)

| Error | Code | Resolution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `QUOTA_OVERCOMMIT` | 1400 | Sum of tenant quotas exceeds database quota, or database quota exceeds global; rebalance quotas |
| `TENANT_QUOTA_EXCEEDED` | 1401 | Tenant's rate/concurrency/memory limit exhausted; reduce load or raise quota via `ALTER TENANT … IN DATABASE … SET QUOTA` |
| `DATABASE_QUOTA_EXCEEDED` | 1402 | Database's rate/concurrency/memory limit exhausted; reduce load or raise quota via `ALTER DATABASE … SET QUOTA` |
| `SERVER_OVERLOAD` | 1403 | A dispatch queue is at capacity. Nothing ran. Retry with exponential backoff. See [Transient capacity refusal](#transient-capacity-refusal-server_overload). |
| `TENANT_VECTOR_DIM_EXCEEDED` | 2010 | Vector embedding dimensionality exceeds tenant's `max_vector_dim`; reduce dimensionality or raise quota |
| `TENANT_GRAPH_DEPTH_EXCEEDED` | 2011 | Graph traversal depth exceeds tenant's `max_graph_depth`; reduce traversal depth or raise quota |

A login past the cluster session cap (`max_active_sessions`) answers `BAD_REQUEST` (5001). Close idle sessions or raise the cap.

## Transient capacity refusal (`SERVER_OVERLOAD`)

The bridge dispatcher refuses a request when one of its capacity limits is full:

| Scope | Limit |
| --- | --- |
| `tenant_inflight` | The tenant holds its fair share of in-flight requests. The share is the total queue capacity divided by the tenants with requests in flight, and at least two. |
| `database_suspended` | The database's queue on the target core holds 95% or more of its fair share. |
| `queue_full` | The target core's queue holds its full capacity. |

The refusal is transient and retryable:

- The server enqueued nothing and ran nothing. Resending the same request is safe.
- Retry with exponential backoff. The same request succeeds once in-flight requests finish and free capacity.
- The error message names the scope and its counts.

Each protocol answers the refusal with one class:

| Protocol | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| pgwire | SQLSTATE `57P03` (`cannot_connect_now`) |
| Native | Error frame with SQLSTATE `57P03` and `ndb_code` 1403 |
| HTTP | `503 Service Unavailable`, `Retry-After: 1`, body `code` `NDB-1403` |
| RESP | `BUSY` error prefix |

`57P03` is distinct from `XX000` (internal error). A client backs off and retries on `57P03`, and aborts on `XX000`. A collection that is draining and a migration in progress also answer `57P03`, with the same retry contract.

Two counters track the refusal:

- `nodedb_dispatch_capacity_busy_total{scope}` counts each refusal by scope, including the ones the server waits out before it answers. It measures dispatch pressure.
- `nodedb_server_overload_responses_total{protocol}` counts each overload answer a client receives. It measures client impact.

A gap between the two means the server absorbs the pressure. See [Monitoring](../administration/monitoring).

A cross-shard (Calvin) transaction does not fail on this refusal. The Calvin scheduler holds the refused transaction with its locks and re-sends it once capacity frees. `nodedb_calvin_dispatch_deferred_total{vshard}` counts those deferrals.

## Database Clone Errors (1500–1599)

| Error | Code | Resolution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `CLONE_DEPTH_EXCEEDED` | 1500 | Clone lineage exceeds `MAX_CLONE_DEPTH = 8`; promote source clone to materialize and flatten lineage |
| `CLONE_DEPENDENCY` | 1502 | Source database has dependent clones; `DROP` clones first or use `DROP DATABASE source FORCE` to trigger materialization |
| `CANNOT_CLONE_MIRROR` | 1501 | Mirrors are read-only and create ambiguous bitemporal lineage; `PROMOTE` the mirror first, then clone |

## Database Mirror Errors (1700–1799)

| Error | Code | SQLSTATE | Resolution |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `MIRROR_READ_ONLY` | 1700 | `25006` | Mirror is read-only until promoted; use `ALTER DATABASE <mirror> PROMOTE` to switch to writable mode |
| `STALE_READ_NOT_LEADER` | 1701 | `55P03` | `Strong` consistency requested on a mirror (which is stale); use `BoundedStaleness` or `Eventual` consistency, or connect to the source database for strong reads |

A lagging or disconnected mirror is a mirror status (`Degraded`, `Disconnected`), not an error. Queries against it keep the codes above.

## Move Tenant Errors

| Error | Code | Resolution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `MOVE_TENANT_ALREADY_AT_TARGET` | 1604 | Tenant already moved to target database; re-issuing is idempotent (safe to retry) |

## Session Lifecycle Errors

Session lifecycle conditions carry no dedicated code. Each answers the code and SQLSTATE below, and the message names the condition.

| Condition | Code | SQLSTATE | Resolution |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Session closed: user account dropped | `BAD_REQUEST` (5001) | `42601` | Reconnect with a valid user |
| Session closed: idle timeout exceeded | `BAD_REQUEST` (5001) | `42601` | Reconnect; keep the session active within `idle_session_timeout_secs` |
| Session closed by `KILL SESSION` | `BAD_REQUEST` (5001) | `42601` | Reconnect |
| Session closed: OIDC token expired | `AUTH_EXPIRED` (2001) | `28000` | Re-authenticate with a fresh token |
| `KILL SESSION` names an unknown session ID | `UNDEFINED_OBJECT` (1111) | `42704` | Check active session IDs with `SHOW SESSIONS` |

## Error Construction

Errors are created via constructors: `NodeDbError::storage("detail")`, `NodeDbError::collection_not_found("users")`, etc.
